content.thinking:
In `AppStartupRuntime`, `engine.start()` is called with two callbacks:
1. `(deltaTime) => { uiSetup.update(deltaTime); onShaderUpdate(deltaTime); }` - the render update callback
2. `() => { onOrbitUpdate(); }` - the render callback (could be post-render)

And `onOrbitUpdate` is the orbit controls update. Let me see what `engine.start` signature looks like and what the callbacks mean.

For `WorksTheatreBridge`, it calls:
```javascript
engine.start(() => undefined, () => undefined);


```

Both callbacks do nothing, so the orbit controls `update()` is never called in the render loop. With `enableDamping = true`, Three.js OrbitControls requires `update()` to be called each frame to apply velocity changes from mouse input—without it, damping won't work and the camera might not respond correctly to interaction.

```
Ah, so the real issue is that `controls.update()` is never being called in the render loop, which means OrbitControls can't actually apply the rotation and panning to the camera even though the event listeners are capturing input. And there's a timing problem too—the controls are instantiated after `engine.start()` is called, so those initial callbacks can't reference them anyway.
```

javascript
engine.start(() => undefined, () => undefined);

const renderer = engine.getRenderer() as { domElement?: HTMLCanvasElement } | null;
const domElement = renderer?.domElement;
if (!domElement) return;
this._controls = new OrbitControls(engine.getCamera(), domElement);
```

I need to look at what the Engine.start() method actually expects from those two callbacks to figure out where to hook in the controls update.

